WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:33.666
Hi, my name is Joan Steitz. I'm a professor at Yale.

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:37.332
And what I'd like to tell you about today is the importance of

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:41.732
serendipity in the discovery process in science.

00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:45.599
The story I'm going to be telling you has to do with SNURPs

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:53.032
which are little particles inside all of our cells that are the building blocks of the spliceosome

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:57.166
which assembles on pre-messenger RNAs to remove the introns

00:00:57.167 --> 00:01:01.332
and make the messenger RNA that can be translated by the ribosome.

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:04.466
And these little SNURPs, as you see in this slide,

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:09.299
have both RNA molecules and protein molecules in them.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:17.799
The other way in which this story is unusual is that it's definitely not a bench to bedside story.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.499
Instead it's very much a bedside to bench story

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.999
where we made use of something that physicians had known about for a long time

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.366
in order to make discoveries about the basic workings of cells.

00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:39.832
So the story starts in the early 1970s and at that time of course we knew about the central dogma.

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:43.999
We knew that DNA made RNA, and RNA made protein.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.132
What we didn't know about was introns or splicing.

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:54.232
People had worked out many of the steps in gene expression in bacterial cells

00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:59.232
and most people thought that higher cells would be sort of similar.

00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:03.566
Perhaps a little bit more complicated but no new principles.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.466
How wrong we were!

00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:09.099
What we did know in the early 1970s

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.632
was that there were several things that were peculiar about higher cells.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:19.066
We knew that they had way too much DNA, a hundred to a thousand times more than

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:24.266
we thought they would need in order to encode the number of genes that we thought we had.

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.466
And the other things was that RNA,

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:30.699
lots of it got made in the nucleus but about ninety percent of it

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:37.632
got degraded and only ten percent of it ever made it out to the cytoplasm to be a messenger RNA.

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:39.532
So, what was going on?

00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:42.066
And as a young assistant professor at Yale,

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:46.699
I had studied RNA structure and function in bacteria and bacteriophages

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:52.299
and I decided I wanted to take on this problem on my first sabbatical leave.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.332
We knew that there were RNA binding proteins inside mammalian cell nuclei

00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:03.932
that immediately bound to the newly synthesized messenger RNA and I thought if I had

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:09.666
antibodies against those proteins, I could use them as tools and maybe this would help me

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:16.199
figure out how the cell decides which molecules to degrade and which molecules to send out

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.966
to the cytoplasm as messenger RNAs.

00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:23.666
So, for half a year, I tried to make antibodies

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:28.232
against these nuclear RNA binding proteins but this was without success

00:03:28.233 --> 00:03:33.566
because these are very highly conserved proteins and therefore very non-immunogenic.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:37.332
So I gave up and did something else for the rest of my sabbatical.

00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:43.166
But it was the year 1977 in the summer that introns were discovered.

00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:46.866
Evidence came together from laboratories from all over the world

00:03:46.867 --> 00:03:53.866
that conclusively convinced everyone that our genes, in fact, were not like bacterial genes

00:03:53.867 --> 00:03:58.099
but contained segments of nonsense, the so-called introns.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:05.099
And that these introns were then spliced out once the entire gene was made into pre-messenger RNA

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:10.366
and that was what enabled the cell to send a fully formed message

00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:14.032
`to the cytoplasm to be translated by the ribosomes.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:20.232
So the question then was: what is the cellular machinery that very precisely recognizes

00:04:20.233 --> 00:04:27.232
the ends of the introns and allows the splicing process to be exact?

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:32.366
When I went back to my lab in the fall of 1977 everybody wanted to work on this process.

00:04:32.367 --> 00:04:38.166
We were an RNA lab, but frankly we were pretty clueless about what we should do next.

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:45.432
And then came the first piece of serendipity, in January of 1978 a new issue of the journal Nature

00:04:45.433 --> 00:04:51.332
arrived in the lab with this rather complicated title, but the important point

00:04:51.333 --> 00:04:56.632
was the underlined sentence here which says that patients with MCTD,

00:04:56.633 --> 00:04:58.866
mixed connective tissue disease,

00:04:58.867 --> 00:05:03.932
have antibodies against nuclear RNA protein complexes.

00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:09.699
And this caught my attention because while I was trying to make antibodies and failing

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.199
several people had said that they thought they'd heard of some diseases where

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.332
patients made auto-antibodies, antibodies against their own cellular components,

00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:24.332
that reacted with nuclear RNA protein complexes.

00:05:24.333 --> 00:05:28.899
At that time I had in the lab a new M.D./Ph.D. student, Michael Lerner.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.132
He was fresh from his medical school courses and I said,

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:35.500
"Michael, do you know anybody here at Yale Medical School

00:05:40.633 --> 00:05:40.666
who might have patients with these diseases? And he said, ""Sure, I'll go see Harden."""

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:44.232
Harden was right across the street in the rheumotology section

00:05:44.233 --> 00:05:47.332
and that very afternoon Michael came back

00:05:47.333 --> 00:05:53.399
with several small vials of serum from patients with suspected auto-antibodies.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.099
Now, the sobering part of this story is that if this were to happen today,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:05.166
one would have to spend weeks if not months filling out human investigation forms,

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:11.766
getting it through committees in order to use even just a few milliliters of serum

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:13.832
`from a patient in an experiment.

00:06:13.833 --> 00:06:15.832
So, this probably never would have happened.

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:21.532
But in any case, Michael started working with these sera and the idea was that the auto-antibodies

00:06:21.533 --> 00:06:26.132
would then recognize the cellular component and we would use

00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:33.799
them as tools to both characterize the, to purify and to characterize the function

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.566
of the target of the auto-antibodies.

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:42.799
And here, what you see in this picture is the idea that maybe RNA was in there and protein as well.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.166
And we now know that those were SNURPs and that patients with lupus

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:51.566
and other autoimmune diseases often make antibodies against SNURPs.

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:59.632
However, the going was rough.  As Michael purified, the cellular component kept disappearing.

00:06:59.633 --> 00:07:01.732
We now know because ribonuclease

00:07:01.733 --> 00:07:07.032
was in the preparations and kept eating away at the RNA component.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:10.232
And then the second piece of serendipity happened.

00:07:10.233 --> 00:07:17.332
And this was a seminar visit by Joan Brugee who is now professor at Harvard Medical School.

00:07:17.333 --> 00:07:24.799
And she came and talked about a new reagent called Protein A that she was using

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:31.632
to capture immune complexes made with lysates of virus infected cells

00:07:31.633 --> 00:07:34.466
and antibodies against those proteins.

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:38.499
And the advantage of that was it could be done very fast.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:42.999
And you could also look at radioactively labeled cell lysates.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:52.666
And what we see here is that, on the very far side, is the total RNAs in the nuclear extract

00:07:52.667 --> 00:07:54.732
of HeLa cells labeled with P32.

00:07:54.733 --> 00:07:59.232
Going from the very small ones at the bottom, about seventy nucleotides long,

00:07:59.233 --> 00:08:02.532
up to U2, which is about two hundred nucleotides long.

00:08:02.533 --> 00:08:04.799
Michael's own serum, which was used

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.599
as a control in the next lane immunoprecipitated no RNAs, but what you see

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:16.199
on the right-hand side here, is the pattern that he obtained with various

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:22.166
different auto-antibodies from various patients. And for the first time, we could see what the

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:27.732
RNAs were and then we could also use this to find out what the proteins were

00:08:27.733 --> 00:08:31.232
in these little particles.  So, what we now know,

00:08:31.233 --> 00:08:37.132
is that these very highly conserved and very abundant targets of the auto-antibodies are,

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:40.632
in fact, the SNURPs that, by base pairing,

00:08:40.633 --> 00:08:44.966
recognize specific sequences in the pre-messenger RNA,

00:08:44.967 --> 00:08:50.299
gather together with a lot of other factors, to form the spliceosome that carries

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:56.466
out the two-step reaction that's involved in pre-messenger RNA splicing

00:08:56.467 --> 00:09:04.332
to give, finally, the messenger RNA that can be translated into protein.

00:09:04.333 --> 00:09:08.566
This isn't just test tube fiction, here you see a beautiful picture from Anne Byer's lab

00:09:08.567 --> 00:09:14.532
in Virginia, showing DNA with nascent RNA transcripts coming off it,

00:09:14.533 --> 00:09:19.199
particles building up at the five-prime and three-prime ends of an intron,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.632
coalescing to form a spliceosome, with the intron looped out, which will then

00:09:24.633 --> 00:09:30.366
shortly be removed by the actual chemical process of splicing.

00:09:30.367 --> 00:09:34.499
So, what I've told you is about how serendipity

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.766
and being interested in RNA-protein complexes

00:09:37.767 --> 00:09:44.166
in mammalian cells led to the discovery of SNURPs, which contain snRNAs and proteins,

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:49.466
and how that process is essential for making messenger RNA.

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:56.566
So, these snRNAs are, in fact, the first non-coding RNAs that we know to be important

00:09:56.567 --> 00:09:58.666
in the regulation of gene expression.

00:09:58.667 --> 00:10:01.866
Today we have microRNAs, lots, and lots of others.

00:10:01.867 --> 00:10:05.366
And also, as we've understood about the spicing process,

00:10:05.367 --> 00:10:09.566
we know that splicing is the reason why

00:10:09.567 --> 00:10:15.299
we can have the same number of genes in our genome as the fruit fly Drosophila

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:21.299
and yet be more complicated.  And this is because we do splicing in alternative ways,

00:10:21.300 --> 01:00:00.000
therefore, making the most out of every gene, making multiple products from every gene.

